# The low-latency kernel.
#
# What "the low-latency kernel" means on Ubuntu changed, and it is worth being
# precise because the package named linux-lowlatency no longer contains one.
#
# Historically it was a separate kernel flavour: a distinct image built with
# CONFIG_PREEMPT and a 1000 Hz tick. That image still exists on 24.04 as
# linux-image-lowlatency, but only on the 6.8 GA kernel - and on 26.04 it is gone
# from the archive entirely.
#
# What replaced it is the generic kernel plus boot-time tuning. The generic
# kernel is now PREEMPT_DYNAMIC, so its preemption model is a boot parameter
# rather than a compile-time choice, and Canonical ships a small package,
# lowlatency-kernel, whose entire contents is one GRUB snippet:
#
#   /etc/default/grub.d/99-lowlatency.cfg
#   GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT="$GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT preempt=full rcu_nocbs=all"
#
# Both entries below resolve to exactly that arrangement on kernel 7.0:
#
#   noble     linux-lowlatency-hwe-24.04 -> linux-image-generic-hwe-24.04
#                                           + lowlatency-kernel
#   resolute  linux-lowlatency           -> linux-image-generic
#                                           + lowlatency-kernel
#
# So the two supported releases end up on the same kernel and the same
# preemption model. 26.04 is the newer base and has a much newer plugin library;
# 24.04 is what has been in service longer. Neither is a compromise on latency.
#
# If you specifically want the old PREEMPT flavour rather than a tuned generic
# kernel, it exists only on 24.04 and only at 6.8:
#
#   sudo apt install linux-lowlatency     # on 24.04 only; installs 6.8
#
# That is a real trade - a purpose-built image against two years of kernel work -
# and docs/DESIGN.md argues why the tuned 7.0 generic is the better default.
linux-lowlatency-hwe-24.04   [noble]
linux-lowlatency             [resolute]

# Firmware for whatever USB and network hardware the box turns out to have.
linux-firmware
